## Final Judgement
**Summary of key insights:**  
The fact-check reveals that the claim's "22 million jobs lost" figure aligns closely with the household survey measurement of 22.4 million jobs lost from February to April 2020, though the more commonly cited nonfarm payroll data shows a smaller decline of about 20.5 million jobs. However, no evidence was found regarding the "11.6 million jobs added back" portion of the claim.

**Missing information:**  
We lack data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics or other authoritative sources confirming that 11.6 million jobs were added back by the claim date (October 7, 2020).

**Conclusion:**  
While the job-loss component of the claim is partially supported by household survey data, the critical job-recovery figure (11.6 million) remains unverified by the recorded evidence, preventing a full confirmation of the claim. Therefore, based on the available record, the claim is `refuted`.

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The fact-check concludes that Mike Pence's claim about "22 million jobs lost" is partially supported, as the household survey measurement shows a decline of 22.4 million jobs from February to April 2020, while the nonfarm payroll data indicates a loss of 20.5 million jobs (sources: [blog.nic.org](https://blog.nic.org/record-decline-of-20.5-million-jobs-in-april)). However, the crucial assertion that "11.6 million jobs" had been added back by October 2020 was not verified within the provided evidence, leading to the verdict of the claim being refuted.